WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.700
A crash dump shows the same function name two thousand times in a row. That is a recursion with no base case eating the call stack.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:11.828
Pause and think.

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:14.841
What two things must every recursive function have?

00:00:15.241 --> 00:00:22.555
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:28.968
This is one of the most important lessons. Recursion, and the call stack that makes it work.

00:00:29.005 --> 00:00:38.072
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:00:38.472 --> 00:00:48.225
New word: recursion. A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller pieces. Picture it like this: Russian dolls: open one to find a smaller one.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:52.868
Imagine Russian nesting dolls.

00:00:53.318 --> 00:00:58.508
To find the smallest doll, you open one, and inside is a smaller version of the same problem.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:03.218
You keep opening until you hit the tiny solid doll. That is the base case.

00:01:03.668 --> 00:01:08.173
Then you close the dolls back up in reverse order. That is the function returning.

00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:15.630
Every function call gets a frame on the call stack, holding its local variables and where to return.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.483
Let us compute factorial of three.

00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:24.065
Factorial of three calls factorial of two. A new frame is pushed on top.

00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:28.300
Factorial of two calls factorial of one. Another frame.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.593
Factorial of one is the base case. It returns one.

00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:37.687
Now frames pop off: two times one is two, then three times two is six.

00:01:39.037 --> 00:01:41.974
On a tree, recursion follows the branches.

00:01:42.424 --> 00:01:47.091
To validate a binary search tree, each call receives an allowed range.

00:01:47.155 --> 00:01:56.048
New word: binary search. Find an item in sorted data by halving the range each step. Picture it like this: Guessing a number with "higher or lower".

00:01:57.698 --> 00:02:03.375
Going left, the upper limit shrinks to the parent value. Going right, the lower limit rises.

00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:07.808
Watch the stack grow as we go down, and shrink as calls return.

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:13.807
At node three, the range is five to six, and three is outside it. So the answer is false.

00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:23.479
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:25.981 --> 00:02:32.831
Every recursive function needs two parts: a base case that stops, and a step that makes the problem smaller.

00:02:33.281 --> 00:02:39.191
The call stack has limited size. A recursion depth of a million can crash with a stack overflow.

00:02:39.641 --> 00:02:44.946
In kernel code the stack is only a few kilobytes, so recursion is usually avoided there.

00:02:45.396 --> 00:02:49.413
You can always convert recursion into a loop with your own explicit stack.

00:02:50.763 --> 00:02:54.212
Backtracking is recursion that undoes its last choice.

00:02:54.298 --> 00:03:04.108
New word: backtracking. Try a choice, explore, undo it if it fails, try the next. Picture it like this: Walking a maze and stepping back at dead ends.

00:03:05.748 --> 00:03:10.124
Choose an option, explore deeper, then un-choose it before trying the next one.

00:03:10.574 --> 00:03:14.940
Subsets, permutations, and N queens all follow this template.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:22.977
In this course every recursive problem has an execution visualizer. Press step and watch frames push and pop.

00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:24.201
Back to the question.

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:27.213
What two things must every recursive function have?

00:03:27.613 --> 00:03:31.317
A base case that stops, and a step that makes the problem smaller.

00:03:32.517 --> 00:03:33.387
Quick recap.

00:03:33.787 --> 00:03:36.109
Each call gets its own stack frame.

00:03:36.509 --> 00:03:38.379
The base case stops the descent.

00:03:38.779 --> 00:03:41.008
Deep recursion can overflow the stack.
